L("E013", "New Note on Law 21: 'An Umpire is justified in ruling that part of a bail had been \"struck off\" if as a result of the wicket being hit any part of either bail has been disturbed from its original position on the top of the stumps.'",
  ST, "clarification_umpire_workability", "high", "stated", "An M.C.C. Committee interpretation of the existing note 'The striker would be out ... if any part of either bail is \"struck off\"': the insert in the 1923 book records that the Committee 'at a meeting held at Lord's on Monday, June 15th, 1931, decided' this ruling (S38-03). It is an interpretation in answer to the meaning of a note. Rait Kerr says this 'least disturbance' custom was later found unsatisfactory and was 'interpreted in different ways by different umpires', and reversed it in 1947 (serial 135; ch.5).", ["S38-03", "S16"],
  sec=UN, secsub="no_cause_stated", date="M.C.C. Committee decision of 15 June 1931 (S38-03); first in the Laws text of the 1932 edition.")
