L("E026", "Law 39: OCR 'tc' -> 'to', 'bo' -> 'be'.", NC, "ocr_noise", "high", "derived", "OCR misreadings only.", ["S18"])
L("E028", "Law 4: ball circumference 'not less than nine inches, nor more than nine inches and one-quarter' -> 'not less than eight and thirteen-sixteenths inches, nor more than nine inches'. The note on a replacement ball also changes: 'It has become the practice to call for another ball at any time, with the Umpire's consent, if the old one has become damaged or if the bowler has lifted the seam...' -> 'In the event of the Umpires agreeing that a ball in use is unfit for play they shall have power to allow another ball (as much as possible like the one discarded) to be substituted.'",
  "EXTERNAL_FORCE", "equipment_manufacture", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 122 (4 May 1927). In ch.4 he says that in 1925 it was found that few first-class balls conformed to the Law, that bowlers complained the gauged standard balls were too large, and that manufacturers had 'unwittingly provided an admirable trial of a smaller ball, which in 1927 was legalised', which he lists among measures to assist the bowler (S16). Numbers agree with the raw OCR of both books. The ball-replacement note is a separate change with no stated cause: a damaged ball is now replaced by umpires' agreement with one like it, and seam-lifting moves to the Bowlers instructions.", ["S16", "S38-02"],
  sec="ENJOYMENT", secsub="bowler_batsman_balance", date="Rait Kerr serial 122: 4 May 1927; the 1939 edition tags Law 4 'Revised 4th May, 1927' (S38-01).",
  note="Ball-replacement note change (unfit ball replaced by umpires' agreement) has no stated cause.")
L("E029", "Note on Law 41 removed: 'The \"five runs\" are to be added to the runs already made when the Fieldsman \"wilfully\" stopped the ball.' The Law itself still said 'whatever runs may have been made, five only shall be added.'",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "The 1932 book drops the note (checked in raw OCR, S38-02), leaving the Law's 'five only' wording alone. No source says whether this was deliberate. In 1936 the Law was changed to add the five runs to runs already made (Rait Kerr serial 127, S16), restoring the sense of the dropped note.", ["S38-02", "S16"], date=ED)
