L("E033", "Law 49 cross-reference: '(See Notes under \"The Batsman,\" page 16.)' -> '(See Note 3 under \"The Batsman,\" page 15.)'.", NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived", "Page/note reference only.", ["S18"])
L("E034", "Note on Law 50: trailing page number '15' removed.", NC, "format_artefact", "high", "derived", "Page number only.", ["S18"])
L("E035", "Law 53 (follow-on): 'leads by 150 runs in a threedays' match' -> 'leads by 150 runs (in Australia 200 runs) in a match of three days or more'. The 150-run limit now covers matches longer than three days; the Australian 200-run limit, previously only in the overseas alterations ('See special alterations which apply to cricket in Australia only'), is printed inside the Law.",
  "ENJOYMENT", "avoid_draws", "low", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 123 (4 May 1927: limit for a three- or more day match). He says the follow-on and declaration Laws 'were primarily directed towards reducing the number of drawn matches' (ch.4, S16), but gives no reason for this extension to longer matches, so the cause is inferred. The Australian figure is not new: the 1923 book refers to the Australian alteration and the 1932 book also prints it in its overseas section (S38-03, S38-02).", ["S16", "S38-02", "S38-03"],
  sec=ST, secsub="elasticity_for_conditions", date="Rait Kerr serial 123: 4 May 1927; 1939 tag on Law 53 'Revised ... 3rd May, 1922: 4th May, 1927' (S38-01); 1922 is probably the Australian alteration.")
L("E036", "Law 54 (declarations): 'In a three days' match, at any time on the second day' -> 'in a match of three days or more, at any time on the second day'. Two notes appear under Law 54: a declaration in the luncheon interval must be made within fifteen minutes, and a morning declaration in time for the other side to choose its roller, 'otherwise an extra seven minutes will be allowed for rolling' (in 1923 these were in the Umpires section with 'ten minutes').",
  "ENJOYMENT", "avoid_draws", "low", "inferred", "Rait Kerr serial 124 (4 May 1927: serial 119 extended to a match of more than three days); ch.4 says the declaration Laws were primarily directed at reducing drawn matches (S16); the cause of this extension is inferred. The notes are a move from the unsegmented Umpires section (S38-03), with ten -> seven minutes following the 1931 cut in rolling time (serial 126).", ["S16", "S38-03"],
  sec=ST, secsub="reorganisation", date="Rait Kerr serial 124: 4 May 1927; 1939 tag on Law 54 includes '4th May, 1927' (S38-01).")
L("E037", "Law 6 (wickets): width 'eight inches' -> 'not less than eight inches nor more than nine inches'; stumps 'twenty-seven inches' -> 'not less than twenty-seven inches nor more than twenty-eight inches out of the ground'; bails 'four inches' -> 'not less than four inches nor more than four inches and a half'. New note: in all first-class cricket and in matches between teams selected by governing bodies the wicket shall be nine inches wide, stumps twenty-eight inches, bails four and a half inches.",
  "ENJOYMENT", "bowler_batsman_balance", "high", "stated", "Rait Kerr serial 125 (6 May 1931). In ch.4 under 'Other measures adopted to assist the bowler' he records that a larger wicket failed in 1903 and that the wicket 'was increased in 1931 to twenty-eight inches by nine inches after extensive trials at home and overseas', as part of legislation 'consistently directed towards giving more assistance to the bowler' because of improved pitches (S16). Numbers agree with the raw OCR of both books.", ["S16", "S38-02"],
  sec="EXTERNAL_FORCE", secsub="pitch_technology", date="Rait Kerr serial 125: 6 May 1931; 1939 tag on Law 6 'Revised 6th May, 1931' (S38-01).")
L("E038", "New Note on Law 8: 'The distance shall be 4 feet from the centre of the stumps to the inside edge of the popping crease.'",
  ST, "codification_definition", "medium", "inferred", "Defines how the Law's 'four feet from the wicket' is measured; not in the 1923 book (S38-03). No source gives a reason.", ["S38-02", "S38-03"], date=ED)
L("E039", "Law 9 (the ground) and notes: rolling before each innings and day's play 'not more than ten minutes' -> 'not more than seven minutes' (Law and note); week-end starts: 'the wicket may be mown on the Monday morning' -> 'the wicket shall be mown ... on the first day on which the match is resumed'; covering: 1923 'Pending an alteration to this Law the M.C.C. have advised the County Cricket Clubs...' with a limit 'not ... a larger area than 18ft by 12ft at each end' -> 1932 'The M.C.C. have advised that before and after the actual commencement of play the ground may be protected...' keeping only 'not more than 3ft 6in in front of the popping crease'.",
  UN, "no_cause_stated", "medium", "none", "Rolling time cut: Rait Kerr serial 126 (6 May 1931) gives no cause (S16 ch.6; nothing in ch.4). The covering note drops the 18ft by 12ft area limit and allows covering before as well as after the start; the mowing note becomes mandatory ('shall') and applies to any resumption day. No source explains these; the 1932 preface gives only the general aim (S38-02).", ["S16", "S38-02"],
  sec=SW, secsub="modal_strength", date="Rait Kerr serial 126: 6 May 1931; 1939 tag on Law 9 'Revised 6th May, 1931' (S38-01).", integrity="ok",
  note="ten/seven checked in raw OCR of both books.")
